`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner;

import java.net.URI;

import net.officefloor.plugin.variable.Val;
import net.officefloor.web.ObjectResponse;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.mapper.OwnerMapper;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model.Owner;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto;

public class RespondWithOwnerCreated {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerMapper ownerMapper,
            ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<OwnerDto>> response) {
        OwnerDto dto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
        response.send(ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/owners/" + owner.getId())).body(dto));
    }
}
```

Note the `@Val` OfficeFloor variables thread the value published by the previous step: `ValidateOwnerFields` writes `Out<OwnerFieldsDto> validated`, `BuildOwner` reads it and writes `Out<Owner> built`, and `SaveOwner`/`RespondWithOwnerCreated` read the `Owner`.

## 3. Owner entity + DTOs (telephone field and validation)

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` — the JPA entity. Telephone field and its constraints (lines 46-50):
```java
    @Column(name = "telephone")
    @NotEmpty
    @Digits(fraction = 0, integer = 10)
    @Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]{10}$", message = "Phone number must be exactly 10 digits")
    private String telephone;
```
`address` and `city` are `@NotEmpty`; `Owner extends Person` (firstName/lastName live in `Person`). It has `getTelephone()`/`setTelephone()` (lines 71-77).

**The DTOs are NOT hand-written Java files — they are OpenAPI-generated at build time from** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`. `OwnerFieldsDto`, `OwnerDto`, and `ValidationMessageDto` are generated into the `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto` package (no `.java` source exists under `src/`). To change DTO shape/validation you edit `openapi.yml`. The relevant schema (openapi.yml lines 1991-2039):

```yaml
    OwnerFields:
      title: Owner fields
      description: Editable fields of a pet owner.
      type: object
      properties:
        firstName:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 30
          pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}$"
          example: George
        lastName:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 30
          pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}\\.?$"
          example: Franklin
        address:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 255
        city:
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 80
        telephone:
          title: Telephone number
          description: The telephone number of the pet owner.
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 20
          pattern: '^[0-9]*$'
          example: '6085551023'
      required:
        - firstName
        - lastName
        - address
        - city
        - telephone
    Owner:
      title: Owner
      description: A pet owner.
      allOf:
        - $ref: '#/components/schemas/OwnerFields'
        - type: object
          properties:
            id:
              type: integer
              format: int32
              minimum: 0
              readOnly: true
            pets:
              type: array
              ...
```

So `OwnerDto` = `OwnerFieldsDto` + `id` (readOnly) + `pets`. **Telephone validation lives in two places that currently disagree:** the OpenAPI schema allows `maxLength: 20` and `pattern: '^[0-9]*$'` (any number of digits), while the JPA entity `Owner.telephone` requires exactly 10 digits (`^[0-9]{10}$`). Neither currently strips non-digit characters.

The MapStruct mapper `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` maps `OwnerFieldsDto → Owner` (ignoring `id` and `pets`) and `Owner → OwnerDto`.

## 4. Tests related to creating owners / telephone

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — the active checkpoint tests (currently failing/target behavior). Header comment: *"telephone-normalize: On create, normalize the telephone by removing every non-digit character, then require exa[ctly 10 digits]"*:
```java
	@Test
	void coreStripsToTenDigits() throws Exception {
		ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
		o.put("telephone", "(04) 1234-5678");
		int id = createOwnerOk(o);
		getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.telephone").value("0412345678"));
	}

	@Test
	void errorRejectsTooFewDigits() throws Exception {
		ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
		o.put("telephone", "12345");
		createOwner(o).andExpect(status().isBadRequest());
	}
```
These expect: (a) `POST /api/owners` strips non-digits from telephone and stores `"0412345678"`, readable back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`; (b) a telephone that reduces to fewer than 10 digits yields 400. Neither behavior exists yet — `ValidateOwnerFields` only checks blank/missing, and the entity/schema patterns would currently reject `"(04) 1234-5678"` rather than normalize it.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — the black-box test harness. Key helpers: `ownerNode()` (builds a valid unique owner with `telephone = "04" + 8-digit seq`, lines 117-125), `createOwner(...)` → `POST /api/owners` (155-158), `createOwnerOk(...)` asserts 2xx and extracts id (160-162), `getOwner(id)` → `GET /api/owners/{id}` (189-191), `extractId(...)` reads `id` from body or the `Location` header (206-219). Runs `@SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc @Transactional` as `@WithMockUser(roles={"OWNER_ADMIN",...})`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` — a test support class (referenced by the suite; not owner-creation assertions per se).

## 5. How 400 validation errors are produced (escalation handling)

OfficeFloor maps a thrown exception type to a handler via a YAML file named after the fully-qualified exception class in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/`. There are two relevant 400 paths:

**Path A — the create-owner "missing fields" 400** (what the POST pipeline throws today):
- `ValidateOwnerFields` throws `MissingOwnerFieldsException(missing)` — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/MissingOwnerFieldsException.java` (extends `Exception`, holds `List<String> fields`).
- Escalation mapping `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.MissingOwnerFieldsException.yml`:
  ```yaml
  handle:
    class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler
  ```
- Handler `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler.java` responds 400 with body `{"errors": [<field names>]}`:
  ```java
  public void handle(@Parameter MissingOwnerFieldsException ex,
          ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>>> response) {
      response.send(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
              .body(Map.of("errors", ex.getFields())));
  }
  ```

**Path B — schema/bean-validation 400** (`MethodArgumentNotValidException`, produced when Jakarta/OpenAPI validation on the DTO fails):
- Mapping `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException.yml`:
  ```yaml
  handle:
    class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.ValidationExceptionHandler
  ```
- Handler `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/ValidationExceptionHandler.java` builds an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail` (400) with a `schemaValidationErrors` array — one `ValidationMessageDto` per field error carrying `field`, `rejectedValue`, `defaultMessage`:
  ```java
  ProblemDetail detail = ProblemDetails.build(ex, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST,
          "The request contains invalid or missing parameters");
  ... bindingResult.getFieldErrors().stream().map(fieldError -> {
        String message = "Field '%s' %s (rejected value: %s)".formatted(...);
        return new ValidationMessageDto(message)
                .putAdditionalProperty("field", fieldError.getField())
                .putAdditionalProperty("rejectedValue", rejectedValue)
                .putAdditionalProperty("defaultMessage", defaultMessage);
  })...
  response.send(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(detail));
  ```
- Shared builder `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/ProblemDetails.java` sets `type=about:blank`, `title=<exception simple name>`, `timestamp`, and an empty `schemaValidationErrors` placeholder.

Other escalation mappings in the same directory (for completeness): `org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException.yml` → `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler`; `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.NotFoundException.yml`; `org.springframework.security.authorization.AuthorizationDeniedException.yml`; and the catch-all `java.lang.Exception.yml` → `GeneralExceptionHandler`.

### Summary of what you'd likely edit to implement telephone normalization
- Normalization/strip-and-validate logic belongs in `ValidateOwnerFields.java` (the first pipeline step) — strip non-digits from `request.getTelephone()`, set it back on the DTO before `validated.set(request)`, and throw a 400-mapped exception if the result isn't exactly 10 digits.
- The blocking constraints to reconcile: `Owner.java` entity pattern `^[0-9]{10}$` and the `openapi.yml` `OwnerFields.telephone` pattern `^[0-9]*$` / `maxLength: 20` (raw input `"(04) 1234-5678"` contains non-digits and would fail schema validation before your normalize code runs unless the schema/entity are relaxed or normalization happens earlier).
- 400 responses are already wired via the escalation YAMLs above; the "too few digits" case can reuse `MissingOwnerFieldsException` or a new exception with its own `escalation/*.yml` mapping.

